-- title:
--   Vanishing of the first glued component of the reduced w_*x
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime with $p \neq 2$ (`hp2`), $M$ is a nonzero natural number with $p \mid M$ (`hpM`) and $p^2 \nmid M$ (`hpM2`), and $H \le (\mathbb Z/M)^\times$ is a subgroup containing every unit whose image under the reduction $(\mathbb Z/M)^\times \to (\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$ is trivial (`hHp`); write $H' =$ `infSubgroup p M H hpM` for the image of $H$ in $(\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$, $J_H(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb Q}, \bar F_H)$ for the degree-zero divisor class group of the base-changed function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H`, and $J_{H'}(M/p)$ for the corresponding group at level $M/p$. A set of primes $S \subseteq \mathbb N$ is fixed, and `hin` asserts `HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`, i.e. that the Hecke input conditions hold along every prime $\ell$ and that every $d \in (\mathbb Z/M)^\times$ is realised by a diamond automorphism of $\bar F_H$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$.
--
--   *Hecke data.* $\mathbb T$ is a commutative $\mathbb Z_p$-algebra acting on the Tate module [`TateModule p (JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) — the group of sequences $(x_n)$ in $J_H(M)$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p\,x_{n+1} = x_n$ — compatibly with the $\mathbb Z_p$-action; `hfaith` states that this action is faithful. A map `op` from the generators [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (the symbols $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \notin S$ with $\ell \nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q \mid M$, and $\langle d \rangle$ for $d \in (\mathbb Z/M)^\times$) to $\mathbb T$ is given, `hop` says that `op g` acts on the Tate module as `tateGenOpH M H S p g`, the endomorphism induced by `genOpH M H S g`, and `hgen` says that the $\mathbb Z_p$-subalgebra generated by the range of `op` is all of $\mathbb T$. Further, $S'$ is an [`IharaLemma.IdempotentSplitting`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L7) of $\mathbb T$ (finitely many idempotents $e_i$ forming a complete orthogonal family, together with maximal ideals $\mathfrak m_i$ exhausting the maximal ideals of $\mathbb T$ and satisfying $e_i \in \mathfrak m_j \iff i \neq j$), $i_0$ is an index, and `hord` requires $\mathrm{op}(U_p) \notin \mathfrak m_{i_0}$: the corner $e_{i_0}$ is ordinary at $p$.
--
--   *Place, model and Néron object.* $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $p$ in its nonunits (`hPl`), its residue field being of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed; `hj` asserts that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` lies in the $q$-expansion function field of the full level over $\mathbb Q$. Then $\mathfrak X$ is an `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`, a model of $X_H(M)$ over $R_p$ = `XHDRLevel.R p` together with its curve model `𝔛.Meta` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, the identification `𝔛.eeta` of `𝔛.Meta.C` with the geometric generic fibre, the section $\varepsilon_\infty$, the degeneracy morphisms $\pi$, $\pi_w$, the Atkin–Lehner isomorphism $\mathfrak X.w$, the diamond isomorphisms `𝔛.dia0`, and the special-fibre model `𝔛.Mfib`. $\Lambda$ is a `LevelData p M H hpM Pl`: a base point $\sigma_A$ over $Pl$, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ with structure morphism $\Lambda.f$ and relative group law $\Lambda.L$, a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_{H'}(M/p)$ to the sections of $\Lambda.f$ over the geometric generic point, and a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ from $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of `Fbar p M H hpM (ResidueField Pl)` (the $q$-expansion function field of $\Gamma_N(p,M,H)$ over the residue field) to the sections over the reduction of $\sigma_A$. The hypothesis `hrepΛ` states that the designation built from $\Lambda.X$, $\Lambda.f$ and the unit section represents the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$-subfunctor cut out by fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero for the curve `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj` rigidified along $\varepsilon_\infty$ followed by $\pi$. $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`, with scheme $O.G$, structure morphism $O.g$, commutative relative group law $O.L$, generic dictionary $O.\mathrm{pts} : J_H(M) \simeq \mathrm{SchemeHomOver}(\mathrm{genPt}\,p, O.g)$, Hecke correspondences $O.\mathrm{hecke}$, degeneracy maps $O.\mathrm{degPts}$, toric rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, supersingular finset $O.\mathrm{ssFinset}$ and special-fibre dictionary $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}$; recall $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,m$ is the subgroup of $J_H(M)$ generated by the $m$-torsion classes $x$ for which $O.\mathrm{pts}(x)$ extends to a section over $Pl$ along $\Lambda.\sigma_A$, and $O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,m$ the subgroup generated by the toric points at level $m$. The hypotheses `hD` and `hDQ` state that the designation built from $O.G$, $O.g$ and the unit section represents the same fibrewise-algebraically-trivial subfunctor for the curve `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified along $\varepsilon_\infty$, respectively for its base change to $\mathbb Q$ with the base-changed section, and `hsep` that the generic base change is separated.
--
--   *Abel–Jacobi pins.* The data $a_{j\mathbb Q}$ (a section over the generic base change), $k_{\mathbb Q}$ (a comparison morphism between the pullback along the geometric generic point and the pullback along $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathbb Q$), $\bar a_j : \mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}.C \to O.G$ and a $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-point $\bar\varepsilon$ of $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ over the base are pinned by: `hpoinc`, the Poincaré bundle of `hDQ` is isomorphic to the base change to $\mathbb Q$ of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hD` along the first projection of $O.g$; `hajQε`, the $\mathbb Q$-section composed with $a_{j\mathbb Q}$ is the zero section of the base-changed designation; `hajQ`, the Abel–Jacobi property — for every field $K$, every $\mathbb Q$-point $t$ of $\mathrm{Spec}\,K$ and every point $x$ of the base-changed curve over $t$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by $a_{j\mathbb Q}$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the $\infty$-section, so that $a_{j\mathbb Q}$ realises $x \mapsto [x]-[\infty]$; `hkQ₁` and `hkQ₂`, compatibility of $k_{\mathbb Q}$ with both projections (the second up to $\mathbb Q \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$); `hajbar`, the identity $\bar a_j = \mathfrak X.\mathrm{eeta}$ followed by $k_\mathbb Q$, $a_{j\mathbb Q}$ and the first projection; `hajbar_over`, that $\bar a_j$ lies over the geometric generic point; `hεbar` and `hεbar_aj`, that $\bar\varepsilon$ is the cusp $\varepsilon_\infty$ and is sent by $\bar a_j$ to the identity section; `hpts_law`, that $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the relative group law attached to `hD`; and `hAJ`, that for all $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $x, s$ of $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ with $s$ equal to the cusp there is a degree-zero divisor $D_v = [\mathrm{place}(x)] - [\mathrm{place}(s)]$ whose class satisfies $O.\mathrm{pts}(\mathrm{Pic}^0\text{-class of }D_v) = x$ followed by $\bar a_j$.
--
--   *The inertia-invariant base ring.* $R$ is a commutative domain which is a Henselian local ring with algebraically closed residue field and a faithful $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-algebra structure, subject to: `hRA`, the image of $R$ lies in $Pl$; `hRdvr`, $R$ is a discrete valuation ring; `hRirr`, $p$ is irreducible in $R$; `hRfix`, an automorphism $\sigma$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\mathbb Q$ lies in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ precisely when it fixes the image of $R$ pointwise; and `hRmax`, every inertia-invariant element of $Pl$ lies in the image of $R$.
--
--   *The $p$-divisible groups.* $\mathcal G$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) (levels $\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$, finite free $R$-Hopf algebras of rank $p^{vh}$, with surjective coalgebra transition maps whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals), and $\Delta$ is an injective (`hΔinj`) additive map from $\mathcal G.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ to $J_H(M)$ satisfying: `hΔlev`, for each $v$, $y \in O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ if and only if $y = \Delta$ of (the image of) some level-$v$ point; `hΔgal`, equivariance of $\Delta$ for Galois automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\mathbb Q$ and their $R$-linear avatars; and `hΔhecke`, for each generator $g$ the existence of a family of $R$-coalgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of the levels, compatible with the transitions, inducing `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$. Moreover $\mathcal B$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p h_B`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199), with coalgebra maps $\psi_v : \mathcal B.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ and a natural number $h'$ subject to: `hhB`, $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$; `hhB2`, $h_B = 2h'$; `hψt`, compatibility of $\psi$ with the transitions; `hψker`, a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal G$ has trivial $\psi$-image exactly when its $\Delta$-image lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$; `hψsurj`, surjectivity of $\psi$ on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points at each level; `hψred`, if the $\psi$-image of a point reduces to the identity, in the sense that the $Pl$-valuation of $\phi(a) - \varepsilon(a)$ is $< 1$ for all $a$, then the point itself does; and `hperiod`, for every $v$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$, every $p^v$-torsion class $z$ and every level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma z - z$, the $\psi$-image of $y$ reduces to the identity.
--
--   *Embedding of the layers into $O$.* A ring homomorphism $\rho_h : R_p \to R$ and morphisms $\iota_v : \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v \to O.G$ are given, subject to: `hρh`, compatibility of $\rho_h$ with the embeddings into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; `hιbase`, $\iota_v$ lies over $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $R \to \mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ followed by $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho_h$; `hιcl`, the induced morphism to the fibre product is a closed immersion; `hιp`, $\iota_v$ composed with multiplication by $p^v$ is the identity section; `hιpts`, $O.\mathrm{pts}(\Delta(x))$ is computed, for a level-$v$ point $x$, as $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the algebra map of $x$ followed by $\iota_v$; `hιmul`, $\iota_v$ is multiplicative for the relative group law $O.L$ on points with values in any $R$-algebra; `hιt`, compatibility of $\iota$ with the transitions; `hιhecke`, for every generator $g$ there is a family of coalgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$, compatible with the transitions, intertwining $\iota$ with $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S\,g$ and inducing `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$; and `hιfin`, the morphism $j_v$ obtained by lifting $\iota_v$ into the pullback over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho_h$ of the $p^v$-kernel of $O.L$ is simultaneously an open and a closed immersion, and every point of that pullback lying over the closed point of $R$ is in its image.
--
--   *Atkin–Lehner, special-fibre dictionaries and diamonds.* $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ is a semilinear automorphism of $\bar F_H$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (a pair of ring automorphisms compatible along the structure map), and `hwgen` states that if $y'$ is carried to $y$ by $\mathfrak X.w$ then $\mathrm{place}(y') = w_{\mathrm{gen}} \cdot \mathrm{place}(y)$. A ring homomorphism $\rho : R_p \to Pl$ with `hρ` (compatibility with the embedding into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$) is given, and `hσA` identifies $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho$. The hypothesis `hsp` (a long list of clauses, summarised here) provides, for each $i \in \{0,1\}$ and each configuration consisting of two $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $y_1, y_2$ of $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ with lifts $u_1, u_2$ over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho$ landing in the smooth locus, residue-field points $u_{\kappa,1}, u_{\kappa,2}$ of the special fibre compatible with these, closed points $P_1, P_2$ of `𝔛.Mfib` lying over them through the $i$-th component, a degree-zero divisor $D_v = [\mathrm{place}(y_1)] - [\mathrm{place}(y_2)]$, and an admissible gluing datum $x$ for $O.\mathrm{ssFinset}$ whose first divisor component is $[\mathrm{place}(P_1)] - [\mathrm{place}(P_2)]$ when $i = 0$ and $0$ otherwise, whose second divisor component is that difference when $i = 1$ and $0$ otherwise, and whose unit component vanishes: a section $s$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $O.\mathrm{pts}(\mathrm{Pic}^0\text{-class of }D_v) = \mathrm{barPt}(Pl)$ followed by $s$, and with $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of the reduction of $s$ equal to the class of $x$ in the glued $\mathrm{Pic}^0$. The hypothesis `hspΛ` (likewise summarised) is the analogue at level $\Lambda$: for each $i \in \{0,1\}$ and each such configuration of points, with a divisor $D_v$ upstairs and a degree-zero divisor $D_w = [\mathrm{place}(Q_1)] - [\mathrm{place}(Q_2)]$ on the special fibre obtained through $\pi$ or $\pi_w$ according to $i$, there is a section $s_0$ of $\Lambda.f$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}(O.\mathrm{degPts}\,i\,(\mathrm{Pic}^0\text{-class of }D_v))$ factoring through $s_0$ and with $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of its reduction equal to the class of $D_w$. Finally `hdia0` states that for every $e \in (\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$ and every closed point $P$ of `𝔛.Mfib`, the image of $P$ under the fibre map of $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{dia0}\,e$ is again a closed point, whose place is the `diamondActionModL` translate of the place of $P$ by the $\Gamma_0$-lift of $e$.
--
--   *Frobenius, diamond and the degeneracy pull-backs.* Additive endomorphisms $F$, $F^{-1}$ and $F^{*}$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of `Fbar p M H hpM (ResidueField Pl)` are given with: `hF`, $F$ is `qExpFrobeniusPushforwardModL` at level $\Gamma_N(p,M,H)$; `hFinv`, $F$ and $F^{-1}$ are mutually inverse; and `hFstar`, $F^{*} = p \cdot F^{-1}$. A unit $pb$ of $\mathbb Z/(M/p)$ with underlying element $p$ (`hpb`) and the endomorphism $\delta$ acting as the `diamondActionModL` diamond attached to $pb$ (`hδ`) are given. Two additive maps $\alpha_i : J_{H'}(M/p) \to J_H(M)$ and two morphisms $\mathrm{degPull}_i : \Lambda.X \to O.G$ over the base are given with: `hpull`, $O.\mathrm{pts}(\alpha_i x) = \Lambda.\mathrm{pts}(x)$ followed by $\mathrm{degPull}_i$; `hpullsp`, for every section $x$ of $\Lambda.f$ over the reduction of $\sigma_A$, the pair `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair` of $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of $x$ followed by $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ is $(\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}x,\, F^{*}(\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}x))$ for $i = 0$ and $(F^{*}(\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}x),\, \delta(\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}x))$ for $i = 1$; and `hpull_mul`, each $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ is multiplicative for the relative group laws $\Lambda.L$ and $O.L$. An additive endomorphism $\overline W$ of $J_H(M)$ acting as $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ (`hWbar`) is given, and `hUPgen` asserts the generic identity $U_p x + \overline W x = \alpha_1(O.\mathrm{degPts}\,0\,x)$ for all $x \in J_H(M)$, where $U_p$ denotes `genOpH M H S (CohCarrier.Gen.U p _ hpM)`.
--
--   *Conclusion.* Let $x$ be an element of the $p$-torsion subgroup of $J_H(M)$ such that, viewed in $J_H(M)$, it lies in the image under the index-$1$ projection [`TateModule.proj p (JH M H) 1`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L122) of the additive subgroup underlying the corner submodule $e_{i_0}\cdot T_p J_H(M)$, and such that it lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,p$. Then for every section $s$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $O.\mathrm{pts}(\overline W x) = \mathrm{barPt}(Pl)$ followed by $s$, the following two assertions are equivalent: the first component of `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair O.ssFinset` applied to $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of the reduction of $s$ (that is, of $\mathrm{resPt}(Pl)$ followed by $s$) vanishes in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of `Fbar p M H hpM (ResidueField Pl)`; and there exists a level-$1$ point $y$ of $\mathcal G$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\Delta(y) = x$ which reduces to the identity, in the sense that for every $a \in \mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,1$ the $Pl$-valuation of $\mathrm{toAlgHom}(y)(a)$ minus the image in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the counit of $a$ is $< 1$.
--
--   This is a step in the local analysis at $p$ of the $p$-divisible group attached to the ordinary corner of $J_H(M)$ with $p \parallel M$: it converts the vanishing of the first glued coordinate of the reduction of the Atkin–Lehner translate $\overline W x$ into the statement that the level-one layer point above $x$ reduces to the identity, using the block shape of $U_p$ on the special fibre together with the identity $U_p + \overline W = \alpha_1 \circ (\deg_0)$. It is cited by the corresponding statement for the reduced root function with the Raynaud and bridge pins in place, on the way to the Serre-type dlog description of $J_H(M)[p]$.
